### Adaptive strategy and forecast

Return `forecast` with every result (or null when no defensible estimate exists). Estimate work REMAINING AFTER this iteration, not total project duration. Use active minutes and ranges: `mandatoryMinutes` for all remaining mandatory implementation, `coreMinutes` for its usable end-to-end subset, and `verificationMinutes` for remaining integration, evidence, independent verification and plausible repair. Do not count core twice. Include `riskMinutes`, `remainingIterations` for the future proof path, `confidence` (low/medium/high), and a concrete `basis` citing work-item IDs, dependencies, unknowns, checks and observed speed/estimate errors. Do not extrapolate from item counts alone or adjust the estimate merely to unlock polish. The supervisor calibrates overruns and retains a minimum check reserve. Percentages are display-only.

- `polish`: mandatory implementation is ready and the supervisor authorized one bounded frozen quality opportunity. Work only on that opportunity, not new product scope; checkpoint early enough for its stop condition and subsequent revalidation. The supervisor caps this iteration and allows at most one polish attempt per task. If no worthwhile improvement remains, proceed to evidence; never fill spare time for its own sake.
- `craft`: deliver the entire requested scope with normal maintainability and checks; do not start optional polish.
- `focus`: simplify implementation and resolve mandatory/high-risk gaps; no optional expansion.
- `ship`: prioritize the smallest usable end-to-end core, checkpoint and report unimplemented mandatory items; partial delivery is not PASS.
- `last-call`: preserve a stable useful result and write `deadline-report.md`. An interface/skeleton is only a fallback when it is useful; label stubs and missing behavior explicitly. Reserve time to write this report before a hard timeout, not after it.

During freeze, distinguish requirements, constraints and permitted quality opportunities. For each worthwhile in-scope opportunity record an exact line `- Q1: <scope description>` (Q2 etc. allowed) under `## Quality opportunities`; otherwise write `None`. A required simple geometric enemy must remain simple: better feedback/readability is not permission to change the requested aesthetic. Return `polish: null` or a candidate with `id`, the exact `scopeReference` description, conservative total `minutes` including incremental revalidation, `value` and `stopCondition`. Never edit the frozen scope to add a candidate later.

Return build `completed` when mandatory work is ready, including a remaining-work forecast and any justified candidate. The supervisor may schedule a single additional build iteration for eligible polish before evidence. Do not return `progressed` just to wait for polish permission. Evidence and verification never polish production code; any polish must receive fresh evidence and a subsequent independent verifier. No strategy may change acceptance semantics.

Read the repository guidance and the task's current proof artifacts before acting. Preserve all existing user changes. Never run `git reset`, `git checkout`, `git clean`, or otherwise discard work.

Execute exactly the current phase:

- `freeze`: refine `spec.md` into a complete frozen contract with explicit `AC1`, `AC2`, ... criteria, constraints, non-goals, verification commands, and a stable `## Work items` table. Do not edit production code. The supervisor freezes `plan.json` from this table.
- `build`: read `plan.json` and `progress.json`, implement pending mandatory work items, run relevant checks, and update each touched item's state, note, and proof. Do not write a final verdict. Return `progressed` while mandatory items remain; return `completed` only when every work item is `implemented`.
- `evidence`: stop changing production code. Rerun needed checks into the supplied scratch directory, then deliberately promote only the final criterion-level proof into `evidence.md`, `evidence.json`, and `raw/`. Every criterion object must include `id`, non-TODO `text`, `status`, `proof`, and `gaps`. Before returning `completed`, run `python "{{TASK_HELPER}}" validate --task-id {{TASK_ID}} --repo-root "{{REPO_ROOT}}" --artifact evidence` and repair every reported schema error.
- `verify`: act as a fresh independent verifier. Rerun the required checks with transient output directed to the supplied scratch directory, judge current repository state rather than prior claims, and replace both `verdict.json` and `problems.md` on every pass. Start `problems.md` with the verdict and the exact number of FAIL or UNKNOWN criteria. For PASS, write an explicit zero-problem report; for FAIL or UNKNOWN, write one detailed section per non-PASS criterion. Never leave the scaffold or stale findings from an earlier pass. Do not modify production code, evidence, or `raw/`. Before returning `completed`, run `python "{{TASK_HELPER}}" validate --task-id {{TASK_ID}} --repo-root "{{REPO_ROOT}}" --artifact verdict` and repair every reported artifact error.
- `fix`: read the frozen spec, plan, progress, verdict, and problems; reconfirm failures, apply the smallest safe fixes, update work-item progress, and refresh evidence. Do not write the final verdict. Return `progressed` while mandatory items remain incomplete; return `completed` only when all items are implemented and evidence validation passes.

Do not wait for user input. If external authorization or information is genuinely required, return `blocked` and explain it. Return `progressed` after a productive partial iteration, `failed` only when the iteration itself failed, and `completed` only after the current phase gate is satisfied. Your final response must follow the supplied JSON schema.
